  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThere's little change in the day-to-day forecast as high pressure \nremains in control through at least early next week. This keeps \ntemperatures 5-10 degrees above normal, with triple digits in the \nlower elevations each afternoon. Moisture will also remain elevated, \nwith PWATs around an inch through the rest of the week. \nUnfortunately, this combination will lead to some very uncomfortable \ndays ahead, as evaporative coolers struggle to work efficiently and \novernight lows refuse to cool, remaining 10-20 degrees above \nseasonal norms. As a result, a Heat Advisory has been issued for the \nlower elevation zones of eastern Utah and for the Grand Valley and \nthe Lower Yampa River Basin, lasting through 6AM Tuesday morning.\n\nAdditionally, diurnal heating will act on the ample moisture in \nplace to fire convection over the higher terrain. Storm motion will \ncontinue to be slow, keeping the threat of heavy rain and flash \nflooding in the forecast, especially over sensitive terrain and \nrecent burn scars. Lightning and gusty outflow winds will also be \nthreats with any storms. This convection could move out over the \nadjacent valleys later in the day, before activity starts to dwindle \nafter 9PM or so. Remember to stay up to date with the forecast if \ntravelling across the region, especially across the higher terrain, \nand have multiple ways to receive urgent warnings.\n\nThe high begins to shift south and westward through the latter half \nof the week as a weak upper level trough tracks across the Northern \nRockies. Some jet energy associated with the passing wave could \ncreate an environment favorable for strong thunderstorms, with \npossible large hail and stronger outflow winds. Stay tuned to see \nhow this develops over the coming days.",
